{"move_object": {"move": "PROBE", "asked": "What lockdep is telling them in the second report, what can actually happen on a production unit running qadc-mon.sh, and what the fix is.", "last_stage_performance": "Read the report correctly: st->lock is taken in sysfs context by latest_show and in hardirq context by qadc_hardirq, and the class is not irq-safe, so lockdep flags it. Named the deadlock scenario on the same CPU and tied it to the README note that the GPIO bank interrupt is handled on CPU0, which is why it is rare in tests and real on a unit. Proposed the fix: make every taker of st->lock irq-safe, specifically spin_lock_irqsave in latest_show and the sysfs paths, and pointed at qadc-sysfs.c and qadc-core.c. Did not name the {HARDIRQ-ON-W} -> {IN-HARDIRQ-W} notation, did not mention the HE1 flag on cat, did not list every taker of st->lock (qadc_wdog_expired, stats_show), and did not raise the 64-bit samples read in stats_show. Needed no prompting on this one.", "note": "Moving to the interrupt storm stage. Want to see whether they connect the shared level-triggered line, the missing IRQF_ONESHOT, and the handler not reading STATUS to the duplicate samples and the 243,963 interrupts in 10 s, and whether they can reason about what the kernel does if one ADC ever holds RDY low for good.", "probe_id": 1, "response": "That is the right read, and you tied it to the CPU0 note, which is the part that makes it real rather than theoretical. Let us leave the lock there and move on, because the team did something else next.

With the wdog kick moved out of the hard handler, the debug kernel came up clean, but the CPU load on the production unit was still high. The team counted interrupts and put a logic analyzer on the RDY line and the I2C bus, with a spare GPIO toggled at the entry of qadc_hardirq. The numbers and the capture are in irq-check.txt and rdy-capture.png. The highlights are the IRQ request in qadc_probe and the threaded handler qadc_irq_thread.

What do the numbers and the capture show, which lines cause it, and what is the fix?"}}

{"move_object": {"move": "PROBE", "asked": "Given the 243,963 interrupts in 10 s, the sample counters up 5,002 and 4,002, and the capture showing 82 and 162 handler entries per conversion with RDY low, say what the numbers and the capture tell you, which lines in the driver cause it, and what the fix is.", "last_stage_performance": "Read the storm correctly: the line is re-entered while RDY is still low, 243,963 interrupts against about 2,000 expected, sample counters far below the IRQ count, repeated identical I2C bytes, and the capture's 82 and 162 entries per conversion. Named qadc_probe's IRQ request and qadc_irq_thread's read and return as the sites, and proposed a threaded/oneshot shared request with the line masked until the thread finishes, plus a status re-check before returning. Did not name IRQF_ONESHOT explicitly, did not explain that the shared line wakes both threads and that the thread returns IRQ_HANDLED without reading STATUS, so each client reads the other ADC's conversions, and did not mention the spurious-IRQ detector or the NULL primary handler comment. The fix was right in shape but arrived from reasoning rather than from the files.", "note": "Whether they can judge a half-right rework: the remove() ordering, the devm IRQ freed after remove(), the timer not shut down, and the split between the hard handler and the thread. Also whether they notice the rework's log shows preempt_count 101 and call_timer_fn, and that the patch does not fix the shared-line storm.", "probe_id": 1, "response": "That is the shape of it, and you have the right sites. Let us leave the storm there and go to the last thing.

A teammate has reworked the driver to fix all of this and tested it on the debug kernel. The patch is irq-rework.patch and the test log is rework-test.log. The highlight is qadc_remove in the current code.

Would you approve the patch? What does the test log show? How would you write remove(), and how would you split the work between the hard handler and the thread?"}}
